# Netty quantifier laws — '∀' and '∃' with an explicit domain.
#
# The document's grammar writes a quantified expression
# 'quantifier identifiers : expression · expression', and has no abbreviated form
# that leaves the domain out, so every law here writes one. A quantifier is the
# weakest thing in the grammar: its body runs to the end of the expression, which
# is why '(∀x: d· b) ≡ …' needs its brackets and '∀x: d· b ∧ c' does not.
#
# 'x' is bound by the quantifier and is not a law variable; 'd', 'b' and 'c' are.
# A law about '∀x: d· b' reads a line about '∀i: nat· i ≥ 0' — matching renames
# the law's own binder name to the line's, and writes the line's name back into
# the law's other side.
#
# These laws are *not* checked the way 'boolean.laws' is. An assignment of ⊤/⊥ to
# names cannot decide a quantifier, so 'Law.isTautology' cannot reach them and
# they are transcribed from aPToP and trusted. 'Netty.quantifier_wellFormed' and
# 'Netty.quantifier_isBeyondTheBooleanEvaluator' say exactly that much and no
# more.
#
# Left out on purpose: everything that needs machinery this kernel has not got.
# Instantiation ('(∀x: d· b) ⇒ b' with 'x' replaced by a term) needs substitution;
# the empty-domain and one-point laws need bunches; 'Σ' and 'Π' need the number
# quantifiers; and '(∀x: d· b) ⇒ (∃x: d· b)' is false for an empty domain and is
# exactly the kind of law the evaluator here could not catch.

generalized duality: ¬(∀x: d· b) ≡ ∃x: d· ¬b
generalized duality: ¬(∃x: d· b) ≡ ∀x: d· ¬b

# '∀' distributes over '∧' and '∃' over '∨', for every domain, empty included.
generalized distribution: (∀x: d· b ∧ c) ≡ (∀x: d· b) ∧ (∀x: d· c)
generalized distribution: (∃x: d· b ∨ c) ≡ (∃x: d· b) ∨ (∃x: d· c)

# The unit of the operator each quantifier generalizes: quantifying it says
# nothing, whatever the domain is.
generalized identity: (∀x: d· T) ≡ T
generalized identity: (∃x: d· F) ≡ F
